when making the registrations leave blank spaces between lines; or who make erasures in the writings that hinder their reading or insertions that alter them; or who refuse to provide to the police authorities the reports relating to such registrations; or who in any way impede the free exercise of the cited authorities in investigating any case related to them; without prejudice to the responsibility imposed upon them by article 73 of this code, for the crimes and offenses that may have been or could have been committed by those who have lodged in their establishments, and whose names and other personal data do not appear regularly registered in their records.
3.- The muleteers or carriers, carriage drivers and carters who abandon their animals in the middle of a street, road or square;
4.- Those who obstruct public traffic with their carriages or pack animals;
5.- Those who tie their animals to doors, interrupting passage on the sidewalks;
6.- Those who run carriages or horses in the streets and squares with harm to persons and in violation of the regulations of the public authority;
7.- Those who violate the established rules regarding the load that carts, carriages and animals must carry;
8.- Those who fail to register their carts with the town council, and number them in the place indicated to them;
9.- Those who establish raffles or games of chance in the streets, roads, squares or public places;
10.- Those who sell falsified beverages, without prejudice to more serious penalties, in the event that the beverages contain mixtures harmful to health;

11.- Those who allow madmen or lunatics entrusted to their care, or ferocious or harmful animals, to wander;
12.- Those who do not restrain their dogs, or incite them when they attack or pursue passersby, even if they cause no harm;
13.- Those who throw stones, filth or other projectiles, at houses, buildings or fences belonging to others;
14.- Those who throw filth, stones or other hard objects at passersby;
15.- Those who have on balconies, windows, rooftops or other exterior points of their house, flowerpots or other objects, in violation of police regulations;
16.- Those who without being owners or usufructuaries, or without having the use of a piece of land, or the right of passage, enter it, when the crops are in full production, or when the fruits in shells or ears are ready to be harvested or close to it;
17.- Those who allow livestock or large animals to enter another's planted property;
18.- Those who refuse to accept national coins of good standard, for the value they have in their legal circulation;
19.- Those who in moments of accident, tumult, shipwreck, flood, fire or other calamities, as well as in cases of assault, pillage, flagrant offense, public outcry or judicial execution, being able to do so, refuse to provide the services, assistance or aid required of them by the public authority;
20.- Those who are in the cases of articles 283 and 288 of this code;
21.- Those who sell damaged, spoiled or harmful foodstuffs;

22.- (Modified by Law 583 of October 14, 1941 G.O. 5656). Those who for their own consumption, steal fruits hanging from trees or crops, provided that none of the circumstances provided for by article 388 is present;
23.- Those who exercise without a degree, acts of a profession that requires it;
24.- Those who wear uniforms or other insignia that do not correspond to them;
25.- Those who violate the hygienic or health rules, agreed upon by the authority in time of epidemic or contagion;
26.- Those who failing the orders of the authority, neglect to repair or demolish ruinous buildings;
27.- Those who give public performances without license from the authority, or exceed the one that has been granted to them;
28.- Those who violate the police rules with the production of fetid or unhealthy objects, or throw them into the streets;
29.- Those who throw debris in public places, contravening police regulations;
30.- Those who pile up garbage in destroyed houses; 31.- Pharmacists who, without authorization from the local judge
or the physician, sell poisonous substances;

###### Art. 476

In addition to the fine* indicated by the preceding article, the courts are empowered to impose, according to the circumstances, the penalty of one to three days of arrest, on carters, carriage drivers, coachmen and drivers who are in contravention; on those who violate the regulations that determine the load of carts, or of animals; on sell-

*  See explanatory note.

ers of falsified beverages, on those who throw hard objects and filth.

###### Art. 477.- Shall be seized and confiscated

1.- The implements that serve for games and raffles, and the funds and other objects placed in raffle or game;
2.- The falsified beverages, that are found in the possession of the seller and belong to him; which shall be poured out;
3.- The writings and engravings contrary to good morals: these objects shall be torn up.
4.- The damaged spoiled or harmful foodstuffs; these foodstuffs shall be destroyed.

###### Art. 478

In case of recidivism, the penalty of one to five days of arrest shall be imposed on all the persons mentioned in article 475. Those who recidivate with regard to the establishment of games and raffles in streets, roads and public places, shall be remitted to the correctional court, where they shall be punished with imprisonment of six days to one month, and a fine* of five to fifty pesos.

